=17: 2023 divided by17 is 119, since 17*119=2023. Therefore, 2023≡0 mod17, so 2023*(-3)≡0*(-3)=0 mod17.\n\nTherefore, the primes p where 2023*(-3)≡0 mod p are exactly the prime divisors of 2023 (7,17) and p=3. But p is an odd prime. Hence, the primes p that divide 2023 are 7 and 17, and p=3. Hence, Q_p(x) ≡ Q(x) mod p only for p=3,7,17. But the problem states that there exist infinitely many primes p. However, our analysis shows that only finitely many primes (3,7,17) would satisfy the condition. Therefore, this contradicts the problem\'s condition unless Q(x) is such that the leading term does not contribute. Wait, but if Q(x) = x^{2023}, then for primes p not dividing 2023, namely p=13, the previous calculation shows that the leading term does not satisfy the congruence. However, the problem states that there exist infinitely many primes p. Hence, the only way this is possible is if these primes can be made infinite, which only possible if the leading coefficient\'s exponent p-2 is congruent to 1 modulo p, effectively making the leading term not affect the congruence. Wait, perhaps if (p-2) ≡1 mod p, which is not possible. Alternatively, if the exponent (p-2) ≡1 mod the degree of Q(x)? Wait, this might not be the correct approach.\n\nWait, in the example with Q(x)=x^{2023}, the key is that when we consider Q_p(x), the coefficients are raised to (p-2). If p=2k+1, then (p-2)=2k-1, which is an odd exponent. Hence, for the leading term, we have (x^{2023})^{2k-1} = x^{2023*(2k-1)}. Now, for this to be congruent mod p, which is a prime, to Q(x) = x^{2023}, we need 2023*(2k-1) ≡2023 mod p. Simplifying, 2023*(2k-1 -1) ≡0 mod p ⇒ 2023*(2k-2) ≡0 mod p ⇒ 2023*2(k-1)≡0 mod p. So 4046(k-1)≡0 mod p.\n\nSo for this to hold, p must divide 4046*(k-1). Since k is an integer such that p=2k+1 (since p is an odd prime), then k= (p+1)/2. Therefore, substituting, we get p divides 4046*((p+1)/2 -1) =4046*(p -1)/2.\n\nBut 4046=2*2023=2*7*17^2. So 4046=(2)*(7*17^2). So, p divides 2023*(p -1)/2. Therefore, p divides 2023*(p -1)/2. Since p is prime, either p divides 2023 or p divides (p -1)/2. However, p dividing (p -1)/2 implies that p divides (p-1)/2, which can only happen if (p-1)/2 ≡0 mod p, which would require that (p-1)/2 is an integer multiple of p. But (p-1)/2 < p, so the only way p divides (p-1)/2 is if (p-1)/2 =0, which is not the case. Therefore, the only way for p to divide 2023*(p -1)/2 is if p divides 2023.\n\nTherefore, the primes p for which Q_p(x) ≡ Q(x) mod p are primes p dividing 2023, i.e., 7,13,17, and primes for which p divides (p -1)/2. Since (p-1)/2 is an integer only if p is odd, which they are. However, the (p -1)/2 must be a multiple of p. Let’s see:\n\nFor example, take p=13. Then (13-1)/2=6. So 2023*6 ≡ ? mod13. 2023 ≡8 mod13, as before, so 8*6=48≡48-39=9 mod13. So 48≡9 mod13≠0. Hence, p=13 does not divide 2023*6. Similarly for p=7, (7-1)/2=3. So 2023*3 mod7. 2023 divided by7 is 289, so 2023≡0 mod7. Hence, 2023*3≡0 mod7. Hence, p=7 divides 2023*3. Similarly for p=17, (17-1)/2=8. 2023*8 mod17. 2023 divided by17 is 119, so 2023≡0 mod17. Hence, 2023*8≡0 mod17. Therefore, p=17 divides 2023*8.\n\nTherefore, the primes p for which Q_p(x) ≡ Q(x) mod p hold are primes dividing 2023 (7,17) and possibly others? Wait, for p=3: p=3 is an odd prime. Let’s check if 3 divides 2023*(3-1)/2=2023*2/3*? Wait, earlier our reasoning was that for p=3, the condition holds trivially because Q_3(x) - Q(x) is divisible by 3 regardless of the coefficients. Wait, but in our earlier analysis, we considered that Q_p(x) = a_{2023}^{p-2} x^{2023} + ... + a_0^{p-2}. For p=3, this becomes a_{}^{1}x^{2023} + ... + a_0^{1}, and Q(x) is the original polynomial. Then Q_p(x) - Q(x) is (a_{2023}x^{2023} + ... + a_0) - (a_{2023}x^{2023} + ... + a_0) = 0, which is divisible by 3. So for p=3, the condition holds regardless of coefficients, as expected (since 3 is a prime for which the polynomial is automatically satisfied).\n\nBut according to our previous formula, p needs to divide 2023*something. For p=3, 2023 is not divisible by 3 (since 2023 divided by3 is 674.333...). However, in the formula 4046*((p+1)/2 -1) =4046*( (p-1)/2 ). For p=3, this is 4046*(1)/2=4046*1/2=2023. So 3 needs to divide 2023, which it does not. Yet, in the case p=3, the congruence still holds. Hence, our formula doesn\'t capture p=3. Therefore, there\'s a mistake in the previous reasoning.\n\nWait, perhaps the primes p for which the congruence holds are both the primes dividing 2023 and p=3. But how? From the earlier calculation, for primes other than 3, if p divides 2023*(2k -1)/2 mod p, where k=(p +1)/2. But as with p=13, we saw that 13 divides 2023*6 mod13, but 2023*6 mod13=0 because 2023≡8 mod13 and 8*6=48≡9 mod13≠0. Hence, contradiction. Therefore, our initial approach may be wrong.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if we consider that the leading coefficient is 1 or -1, and the exponent (p-2) for the leading term in Q_p(x) is equivalent to some exponent mod p. Let\'s consider that for the polynomial Q(x) = x^{2023}, then Q_p(x) = (leading coefficient)^{p-2} x^{2023} = x^{2023*(p-2)}. To have Q_p(x) ≡ Q(x) mod p, we need 2023*(p-2) ≡2023 mod p. Simplifying:\n\n2023*(p-2) ≡2023 mod p  \n2023*(p-2) -2023 ≡0 mod p  \n2023*(p-3) ≡0 mod p  \nTherefore, 2023*(p -3) ≡0 mod p.\n\nThis implies that (p -3) ≡0 mod p, or 2023 is divisible by p. Let\'s re-examine:\n\n2023*(p -3) ≡0 mod p  \nThis is equivalent to 2023*(p -3) ≡0 mod p  \nSince 2023 is an integer and p is prime, for this congruence to hold, either:\n\n1. p divides 2023,\n2. p divides (p -3) mod p, which simplifies to p divides 3.\n\nSince p is a prime number, the second condition implies that p=3. Because if an prime p divides (p -3), then p divides 3. Since p is prime and p divides 3, then p must be 3. Hence, the primes p that divide 2023*(p -3) mod p are primes that divide 2023 or p=3. Therefore, the primes satisfying the condition are primes dividing 2023 (7,17) and p=3. Therefore, the primes p=3,7,17. But these are finitely many primes, contradicting the problem\'s statement that there exist infinitely many primes p with the given property. Therefore, our initial assumption must be wrong. \n\nHowever, the problem states: "there exist infinitely many primes p such that ... is an integer for all x∈Z." This suggests that our analysis is missing something. The error may lie in assuming that Q_p(x) - Q(x) must be divisible by p, but in fact, since Q_p(x),Q(x) are polynomials with integer coefficients, Q_p(x) - Q(x) is an integer for any x∈Z. However, the problem states that "is an integer for all x∈Z", so the condition is that this must hold for all integers x. However, for the expression (Q_p(x) - Q(x))/p to be an integer for all x∈Z, it is equivalent to Q_p(x) ≡ Q(x) mod p for all x∈Z. But for this congruence to hold for all x∈Z, it\'s necessary that the coefficients of Q_p(x) - Q(x) are divisible by p. In other words, for each coefficient in Q_p(x) - Q(x), which is (a_i^{p-2} - a_i) for each coefficient. Therefore, for each coefficient, (a_i^{p-2} - a_i) ≡0 mod p for all primes p (infinitely many). Therefore, similar to before, this leads each a_i must satisfy a_i^{p-2} ≡a_i mod p for all primes p. Hence, for the leading coefficient a_{2023}, this requires a_{2023}^{p-2} ≡a_{2023} mod p for infinitely many primes p. Hence, leading coefficient must satisfy same condition.\n\nBut as previously concluded, the only solutions to x^{p-2} ≡x mod p for all primes p are x=0, ±1. However, x=±1 only if x^2≡1 mod p for all primes p, which is impossible as previously discussed. Therefore, only x=0 is possible. Hence, a_{2023}=0. But this contradicts Q(x) being of degree 2023.\n\nAlternatively, if a_{2023}=1, then for primes p not dividing 2023, in order to have x^{2023*(p-2)} ≡x^{2023} mod p, for all x. The exponents must be congruent mod p. So 2023*(p-2) ≡2023 mod p. Which simplifies same as before: 2023*(p -3) ≡0 mod p. Therefore, again, p divides 2023 or p=3. But if a_{2023}=1, then this congruence must hold for infinitely many primes p, but as we saw, only finitely many primes (3,7,17). Therefore, contradiction. Hence, our previous conclusion that all coefficients must be zero seems inescapable. But then Q would be the zero polynomial, which contradicts the problem\'s condition that Q has degree 2023. Therefore, there must be a mistake in reasoning.\n\nAlternative approach: Perhaps rather than thinking about Q_p(x) ≡ Q(x) mod p for infinitely many primes, maybe the problem can be approached using the fact that if a polynomial with integer coefficients is congruent to another polynomial modulo infinitely many primes, then they must be the same polynomial. In this case, Q_p(x) and Q(x) must be the same polynomial. Therefore, for each coefficient, a_{2023}^{p-2} must equal a_{2023}, etc. So, for each coefficient, the equation x^{p-2} =x must have the solutions x=a_i. For this to hold for all primes p (infinitely many), the equation x^{p-2}=x must have x=a_i. But as before, solutions to x^{p-2}=x mod p are x=0,±1. So, each coefficient a_i must be 0,1,-1 for all primes p. But as integers, cannot be 0,1,-1 for infinitely many primes p. Hence, only possibility is a_i=0. Hence, all coefficients must be zero except possibly for a_{2023}= ±1. But then leading coefficient is allowed to be 1 or -1.\n\nBut then Q(x)=x^{2023} or Q(x)=-x^{2023}. Let\'s check if these polynomials satisfy the original condition of there being infinitely many primes p such that (Q_p(x)-Q(x))/p is an integer for all x∈Z.\n\nFor Q(x)=x^{2023}:\n\nQ_p(x) = (a_{2023})^{p-2}x^{2023} = (1^{p-2}x^{2023}) since a_{2023}=1. So Q_p(x)=x^{2023*(p-2)}. Then, Q_p(x) - Q(x)=x^{2023*(p-2)} -x^{2023}. For this to be divisible by p for all x∈Z. \n\nIn order for x^{2023*(p-2)} ≡x^{2023} mod p for all x. We can think of this as x^{2023*(p-2) -2023} ≡1 mod p for all x. But 2023*(p-2) -2023=2023*(p-3). Therefore, x^{2023*(p-3)} ≡1 mod p for all x∈Z. However, this must hold for all x. Let\'s check for x=2. Then 2^{2023*(p-3)} ≡1 mod p. This is only possible if the order of 2 modulo p divides 2023*(p-3). However, this must hold for all primes p. But for example, take p=13. Then 2023*(13-3)=2023*10=20230. The order of 2 modulo 13 is 12 (since φ(13)=12), but 12 divides 20230? 20230 divided by 12 is 1685.833..., which is not an integer. Therefore, 2^{12}≡1 mod13, but 2^{20230}≡(2^{12})^{1685}*2^{10}≡1^{1685}*2^{10}≡2^{10} mod13. 2^10=1024; 1024 divided by13 is 78*13=1014, remainder 1024-1014=10. So 2^10≡10 mod13. Hence, 2^{20230}≡10≠1 mod13. Therefore, x=2 would not satisfy the congruence for p=13. Hence, Q_p(x) - Q(x)/p is not an integer for x=2 and p=13. Hence, Q(x)=x^{2023} does not satisfy the given condition. Similarly, Q(x)=-x^{2023} would have Q_p(x) = (-1)^{p-2}x^{2023}. Since p is an odd prime, p-2 is odd, so (-1)^{p-2}=-1. Therefore, Q_p(x) = -x^{2023}. Then Q_p(x)-Q(x) = -x^{2023} - (-x^{2023})=0, which is divisible by p. Hence, 0/ p=0 is an integer. Therefore, Q(x)=-x^{2023} trivially satisfies the condition, because the difference is 0, which is divisible by any prime p. Therefore, Q_p(x)-Q(x)=0, hence the quotient is 0, which is integer.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial Q(x)=-x^{2023} satisfies the condition that (Q_p(x)-Q(x))/p is an integer (in fact, zero) for all x∈Z and infinitely many primes p (in fact, all primes p). Hence, this polynomial satisfies the problem\'s condition. Similarly, any polynomial where coefficients are 0 except a leading coefficient of -1. However, but wait, the problem states that Q is a polynomial of degree 2023, so leading term must have non-zero coefficient. If we have other coefficients as well, but the problem allows for other coefficients? Wait, but earlier analysis suggests that for Q_p(x) - Q(x) to be divisible by p for infinitely many primes p, all coefficients except the leading one must be zero.\n\nWait, let\'s re-examine the equation. If coefficients a_i for i <2023 must be zero. Because for those coefficients, (a_i^{p-2} -a_i) must be divisible by p for all primes p. For i <2023, if a_i≠0, then as before, this requires a_i^{p-2} ≡a_i mod p, which requires a_i=0,±1 mod p and so on. But as integers, a_i cannot be 0,±1 for infinitely many primes p. Hence, a_i=0. Hence, only the leading coefficient a_{2023} can be -1 or1, and other coefficients are zero.\n\nHence, the possible polynomials are Q(x)=x^{2023} or Q(x)=-x^{2023}. Then the value of Q(2023) would be 2023^{2023} or -2023^{2023}. The largest possible value is therefore -2023^{2023}.\n\nWait, but this contradicts our previous check. Wait, if Q(x)=-x^{2023}, then Q(2023)= -2023^{2023}. Which is a negative number, much larger in magnitude than positive or negative 2023^{2023}. But the largest possible value would be the maximum of the absolute values. However, the problem asks for the largest possible value of Q(2023). Since -2023^{2023} is negative and large, whereas 2023^{2023} is positive and even larger. Hence, the maximum in terms of absolute value is 2023^{2023}. However, the problem does not specify to consider absolute value. If the largest possible value as positive, then it would be 2023^{2023}. But since Q(x)=-x^{2023} is also a solution, and in this case, Q(2023)= -2023^{2023} is a very large negative number. So depending on the definition of "largest possible value". If the question is asking for the maximum possible value (in standard ordering of real numbers), then 2023^{2023} is larger than -2023^{2023}. Hence, the largest possible value is 2023^{2023}. However, this contradicts with the check for Q(x)=x^{2023} when p=13 above. As per the earlier calculation, the congruence does not hold for x=2 and p=13. But how come then Q(x)=x^{2023} doesn\'t satisfy the condition, yet its value at 2023 is supposedly the largest possible.\n\nWait, but the problem states that Q is a polynomial with integer coefficients. If Q(x) = x^{2023}, then the coefficients are 0,0,...,0,1. So the coefficients a_i for i=0,1,...,2022 are zero. Then for each prime p, the polynomial Q_p(x) would be (a_{2023})^{p-2}x^{2023} = 1^{p-2}x^{2023} =x^{2023}. Therefore, Q_p(x) - Q(x)=x^{2023}-x^{2023}=0, hence divisible by p for all x. Hence, the quotient is 0, which is an integer. Wait, but this is always true, hence for all primes p (not just infinitely many). Therefore, according to the problem statement, Q(x)=x^{2023} would satisfy the condition of there existing infinitely many primes p such that (Q_p(x)-Q(x))/p is an integer for all x∈Z. However, in our initial analysis, this seems to require p=3 does not hold. Wait, but earlier calculation showed for Q(x)=x^{2023}, when p=13, the equation 2023*(p-3)≡0 mod p=13. 2023 mod13?\n\nWait, compute 2023 divided by13: 13*155=2015, 2023-2015=8. So 2023≡8 mod13. Therefore, 2023*(p -3)≡8*(10)=80 mod13. 80 divided by13 is 6*13=78, remainder 2. So 80≡2 mod13. Therefore, 2023*(p -3)≡2 mod13, which is not zero. Therefore, for p=13, Q_p(x)-Q(x) is not congruent to Q(x) as polynomials, hence (Q_p(x)-Q(x))/13 is not an integer for some x. But this contradicts the problem statement, which requires that for all x, the quotient is integer. Hence, perhaps Q(x)=x^{2023} does not satisfy the problem condition.\n\nBut the problem states that Q is defined as a polynomial with integer coefficients. If Q(x)=x^{2023}, then Q_p(x)=x^{2023*(p-2)}, which for p=13 would make Q_{13}(x)=x^{2023*11}=x^{22253}. Then Q_{13}(x)-Q(x)=x^{22253}-x^{2023}. For this to be divisible by13 for all x∈Z. But when x=2, Q_{13}(2)-Q(2)=2^{22253} -2^{2023} mod13. But according to Fermat\'s little theorem, 2^{12}≡1 mod13. Therefore, 22253 divided by12 gives 22253=12*1854 +5, so 2^{12*1854+5}≡(2^{12})^{1854}*2^5≡1^{1854}*32≡32 mod12= 8 mod13. Similarly, 2023 mod12=2023-12*168=2023-2016=7, so 2^{2023}≡2^7=128≡128-10*12=128-120=8 mod13. Hence, 2^{22253} -2^{2023} ≡8 -128≡8 -128+13*9=8 -128 +117=8 -1=7 mod13. Hence, not divisible by13. Hence, Q_{13}(2)-Q(2)= number ≡7 mod13, hence not divisible by13. Therefore, Q(x)=x^{2023} does not satisfy the given problem condition, even though it is a polynomial with integer coefficients. Therefore, my previous conclusion was incorrect.\n\nThis suggests that our initial reasoning is flawed. Hence, maybe the only polynomials that satisfy the problem\'s condition are Q(x)=0, but that contradicts the degree requirement. Given this confliction, perhaps the answer is that the largest possible value of Q(2023) is 0, but since the problem states degree 2023, this is impossible. Hence, there\'s a critical error in the earlier analysis.\n\nAlternative Approach:\n\nGiven that Q_p(x) - Q(x) must be divisible by p for infinitely many primes p. Since Q_p and Q have integer coefficients, the difference Q_p(x) - Q(x) is an integer for any x. For this difference to be divisible by p for all x, the coefficients of Q_p - Q must be divisible by p. Therefore, for each coefficient, (a_i^{p-2} - a_i) must be divisible by p for all primes p. Hence, a_i^{p-2} ≡ a_i mod p. This must hold for all primes p (infinitely many). \n\nAs we saw earlier, this implies that either a_i=0 mod p for all primes p (hence a_i=0), or a_i=±1 mod p for all primes p (impossible). Hence, only a_i=0 for all i. However, this contradicts the degree of Q being 2023. Thus, is there any possible non-zero polynomial?\n\nWait, but perhaps if Q(x) is a polynomial where some coefficients are non-zero and others are zero. For instance, if Q(x) is of the form Q(x)=±x^{2023}, then Q_p(x)= (±1)^{p-2}x^{2023}. Since p is an odd prime, p-2 is odd if p is odd, hence (±1)^{p-2}= (-1)^{p-1}*(±1)^{-2}? Wait, no. Wait, (±1)^{p-2} is either 1 or -1, depending on the sign. If Q(x)=x^{2023}, then (±1)^{p-2} is 1 when p-2 is even, which happens when p is odd (since p is prime greater than 2, p is odd), so (±1)^{p-2}=1. Hence, Q_p(x)=x^{2023*(p-2)}. For this to be congruent to Q(x)=x^{2023} modulo p, we need 2023*(p-2)≡2023 mod p. Hence, 2023*(p-3)≡0 mod p.\n\nSince 2023 is an integer, this 